Our changes to Wine Mono 9.4.0 (all MIT, as Mono's class libraries are),
built by ../build-wine-mono.sh into the tarball the Makefile pins:

wine-mono-0001-ndp4-installpath.patch
    The support MSI also writes NDP\v4\Client and \Full InstallPath (both
    registry views). SQL Server Compact 3.5 SP2's installer edits
    [InstallPath]\CONFIG\machine.config and stopped with 1603 without them.

wine-mono-0002-assemblyfolders-v3.patch
    The support MSI also writes .NETFramework\AssemblyFolders\v3.0 and
    \v3.5 (both views), as .NET 3.5 SP1 leaves them: Meedio's setup took
    their absence for .NET 3.5 missing and ran Microsoft's 3.5 SP1 setup,
    which refuses on Windows 10 ("Turn Windows features on or off").

wine-mono-0003-netfx35-feature-files.patch (sg10)
    The support MSI leaves what a machine with the .NET Framework 3.5
    feature on has: NDP\v3.5 InstallPath and CBS, NDP\v2.0.50727/v3.0 CBS,
    NDP\v3.0\Setup\Windows Presentation/Communication Foundation
    InstallSuccess+Version, AssemblyFolders\DX_1.0.2902.0 (Managed DirectX,
    which Mono implements; MeediOS's setup ran DirectX's setup without it),
    the 64-bit view's AssemblyFolders in [ProgramFiles64Folder]; files:
    Framework(64)\v3.5\csc.exe (3.5's compiler: the 2.0 runtime with the 3.5
    libraries) and Program Files (both views)\Reference Assemblies\
    Microsoft\Framework\v3.0 and v3.5 with Mono's 2.0-api reference
    assemblies (System.Core 3.5, System.Xml.Linq, System.ServiceModel...),
    where AssemblyFolders points. build-msi-tables.sh: a top-level
    ProgramFilesFolder/ProgramFiles64Folder in the image is the MSI's own
    folder of that name. Existing prefixes get it through sg-session's
    sg_mono_support_refresh (0.1.0-109), which re-applies the support MSI
    when it changed -- Wine does not while the version stays 9.4.0.

mono-0001-sg-fixes.patch
    System.Core: System.Diagnostics.Eventing.Reader (EventLogWatcher,
      EventLogSession, EventLogReader...), our own; there are no event logs
      to read, but programs that name the types load (athenaNet Device
      Manager's service and tray program).
    WineMono.Security: X509Store Import/Remove/Clear through crypt32 (were
      NotImplementedException): X509Store.Add/Remove reach the system store.
    System: X509Store.Certificates is a new collection each call, as .NET's
      (Remove while enumerating: "Collection was modified").
    System.Configuration: a section the runtime ignores (<startup>) may
      appear twice in a .config, as .NET allows.
    corlib: Environment.UserInteractive is false in a window station that is
      not visible (a service's), as .NET decides it (was always true: a .NET
      service that runs as a console program when interactive stopped).

mono-0002-system-drawing-framework-names.patch,
corefx-0001-brush-nativebrush.patch
    System.Drawing: the private names .NET Framework's System.Drawing has and
      programs reach by reflection -- Graphics.nativeGraphics, Pen.nativePen,
      Brush.nativeBrush (fields), GraphicsPath(IntPtr, int),
      Bitmap.FromGDIplus(IntPtr) -- over the same GDI+ handles. GdPicture
      (AmbirScan's imaging library) failed in its type initializer, and
      AmbirScan with it (David 2026-10-02). Mono's own code keeps
      nativeObject, a property now for Graphics and Pen.

mono-0003-settings-declared-late-and-wmi-event-watchers.patch (sg11)
    System: ApplicationSettingsBase reads a setting a program declares at
      run time (Properties.Add) from the user.config that already has it.
      The provider had loaded the file's value under an untyped property
      of its own; once declared, that value was read through it, could not
      be deserialized and came back null -- DYMO Connect ended on every
      start after its first (XDocument.Parse(null), David 2026-10-05).
    System.Management: an event query's sink needs no stub between
      apartments (WMI runs in the process); ManagementEventWatcher.Start
      threw PlatformNotSupportedException (GetDemultiplexedStub_f), which
      ended DYMO Connect's launcher at every sign-in.

test/mono-fork-test.sh checks each.

wpf-0001-pts-managed-engine.patch
    PresentationFramework: PTS, the engine WPF's flow layout hands pages,
      columns and paragraphs to, in managed code (MS/Internal/PtsHost/
      PtsEngine.cs, our own, written from the interface's declarations in
      Pts.cs and how PtsHost uses them). Wine Mono's PresentationNative has
      no PTS: FlowDocument, RichTextBox, FlowDocumentScrollViewer and
      FlowDocumentReader stopped with EntryPointNotFoundException
      (CreateInstalledObjectsInfo) -- Sonos Desktop Controller among them
      (David 2026-10-02). Paragraphs stack down columns, text breaks into
      lines through the client's FormatLine, containers and subpages lay
      out their own children, finite pages break between columns and
      pages, lists, sections and BlockUIContainer are placed, tables laid
      out row by row (row spans, row heights, breaks between rows),
      floaters and figures in text put at their side (a figure where its
      anchors say) with the lines beside them narrowed (sg8; before, they
      were not drawn). Not yet: text on both sides of one, column
      balancing, repeated table headers, footnotes. PtsCache:
      without Line Services there is no penalty module (optimal
      paragraphs off).

wpf-0002-managed-textformatter.patch
    PresentationCore, Wine Mono's managed TextFormatter (FullTextLine):
      TextHeight, TextBaseline, MarkerHeight/Baseline, DependentLength and
      IsTruncated answered (were NotImplementedException; flow documents
      ask for them); a list item's marker (bullet, number) is drawn; an
      inline object (InlineUIContainer) is measured while its line is
      formatted, not again when drawn or queried (the flow layout can only
      measure it then: InvalidCastException); the paragraph's alignment
      (centre, right) is applied -- centred text drew at the left; a
      line's break is searched with each run shaped once (prefix widths
      summed; the search shaped every prefix again -- 2.8 ms a line in a
      flow document, now well under 1); a right-to-left paragraph's lines
      start at the right, and its carets and bounds are counted from there
      (right-to-left text drew at the left, under the element's mirror).

wpf-0003-composite-font-fallbacks.patch
    PresentationCore's composite fonts (Global User Interface, Sans Serif,
      Serif, Monospace): every family map's Target ends with families a
      Stained Glass OS machine has -- Liberation, DejaVu, Noto CJK,
      Symbola. They named only Windows' (Segoe UI, Tahoma, MS Gothic...),
      so Hebrew, Arabic, CJK and symbols in WPF text were boxes.

wpf-0004-system-printing-local-print-server-and-queues.patch (sg11)
    System.Printing, over winspool.drv (SgSpooler.cs, our own):
      LocalPrintServer and PrintServer (GetPrintQueue, GetPrintQueues,
      the default queue), PrintQueue (name, driver, port, print processor,
      comment, location, status and attributes and every Is... of them,
      jobs, priorities, Refresh), PrintQueueCollection,
      PrintJobInfoCollection and PrintSystemJobInfo (EnumJobs). Every
      member threw NotImplementedException -- LocalPrintServer's
      constructor too, so DYMO Connect's printer discovery stopped there
      and it found no LabelWriter. Not yet: print tickets and capabilities,
      XPS writers, AddJob, installing or deleting queues, property
      dictionaries.

wpf-0005-print-tickets-and-font-family-names.patch (sg12)
    System.Printing: a queue's UserPrintTicket and DefaultPrintTicket are
      its printer's DEVMODE as prntvpt writes it (DocumentProperties,
      PTConvertDevModeToPrintTicket), settable; GetPrintCapabilities(AsXml)
      are prntvpt's PTGetPrintCapabilities. They threw: DYMO Connect's Print
      did nothing.
    DirectWriteForwarder: LocalizedStrings' enumerator started at 1, not
      before the first, so it skipped the first two strings -- a font
      family's FamilyNames was always empty. DYMO Connect's label editor
      takes the first of them as you type, and crashed at the first letter.

wpf-0006-wpf-programs-aware-of-the-system-dpi.patch (sg13)
    WindowsBase: a WPF program is aware of the system DPI, as on the .NET
      Framework, unless its assembly says [DisableDpiAwareness] or its
      manifest chose (per-monitor, for instance) -- done in the Dispatcher's
      static constructor, before the first dispatcher makes its window (a
      window procedure runs at its window's awareness, and WPF does its work
      in the dispatcher's). Wine Mono's runtime left every WPF program
      unaware: Wine drew it at 100% and scaled the picture, soft at 175%
      (DYMO Connect, Sonos on a Surface Pro 7). Gate test/wpf-dpi-test.sh;
      mutant -define:SG_MUTANT_WPF_DPI_UNAWARE.

mono-0004-x509-crypt32-import-attributes-winmetadata.patch (sg15)
    Visual Studio 2022's installer, in the order it stopped:
    System: X509Chain is built by crypt32 (X509ChainImplCrypt32.cs:
      CertGetCertificateChain, judged by CertVerifyCertificateChainPolicy's
      base policy with the chain policy's VerificationFlags), as .NET
      Framework's is: the system's roots, revocation (cryptnet) and time.
      Mono's managed chain threw NotImplementedException from the store's
      CRL list the moment revocation was asked for, and the bootstrapper said
      "Certificate is invalid" (0x80096004) for every manifest.
      SG_MONO_CHAIN=mono gives Mono's chain back. Mono.Security: the Windows
      store's Crls is empty instead of throwing.
    corlib: X509Certificate.Import reinitializes the certificate, as .NET
      Framework's ("X509Certificate is immutable on this platform": its
      catalog signatures); X509Certificate(IntPtr) takes a CryptoAPI
      certificate context (its signer after WinVerifyTrust).
    corlib: Marshal.GetExceptionForHR/ThrowExceptionForHR ignore a success
      code (>= 0), as .NET Framework: the Windows SDK's setup passes
      GetLastWin32Error() (2) after IsWow64Process2 succeeds, and Mono threw
      "Unable to find the specified file" -- the SDK did not install.
    corlib: a newslot method has no parent definition when attributes are
      inherited (MonoCustomAttrs.GetBase), as RuntimeMethodInfo.
      GetParentDefinition on .NET Framework: VS Installer's options implement
      an interface's Quiet over a base class's Quiet, both with the same
      AllowMultiple attribute, and GetCustomAttribute threw
      AmbiguousMatchException before it read its command line.
    runtime: a reference marked ContentType=WindowsRuntime ("Windows,
      Version=255.255.255.255") loads lib/mono/4.5/WinMetadata/<name>.winmd,
      as the CLR looks in System32\WinMetadata (wine-mono-0004).

corefx-0002-cms-certificate-choices.patch (sg15)
    System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs: a CMS certificate set may hold the
      other CertificateChoices ([0] to [3]); they are kept and not listed
      in Certificates. Microsoft's time-stamp tokens carry an attribute
      certificate there, decoding threw "ASN1 corrupted data", and VS
      Installer took its channel manifest's signature for invalid.

wpf-0007-package-part-streams-seek.patch (sg15)
    WindowsBase: a part read from a package seeks (a deflated entry is opened
      again and read up to the place going back), as .NET Framework's do; VS's
      bootstrapper seeks every part it extracts from vs_installer.opc and got
      NotSupportedException.

wine-mono-0004-winmetadata-windows-assembly.patch (sg15)
    The "Windows" metadata assembly, sg-winmd/ -> lib/mono/4.5/WinMetadata/
      Windows.winmd: our own implementation of Windows Runtime types .NET
      Framework programs call, starting with Windows.Networking.Connectivity
      (NetworkInformation.GetInternetConnectionProfile, a profile's
      ConnectionCost: Unrestricted, what Wine's INetworkCostManager says).
      VS Installer asks the connection's cost and stopped with "Could not
      load file or assembly 'Windows, Version=255.255.255.255'".
    Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg15 section); the sg14 build fails its new
      checks.

mono-0005-variant-missing-dbnull.patch (sg16)
    corlib Variant.SetValue: Type.Missing becomes VT_ERROR
      DISP_E_PARAMNOTFOUND and DBNull VT_NULL, as .NET Framework marshals
      them. An omitted optional argument went to COM as a VT_DISPATCH of
      the Missing object: Visual Studio's installer calls
      ITaskService.Connect() with no arguments, and Wine's Task Scheduler
      refused the "server" (ERROR_BAD_NETPATH): "Failed to create scheduled
      task: BackgroundDownload" 0x80070035.
    Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg16 section); the sg15 build fails its
      three new checks.

mono-0006-safearray-records-ccw-null-name-target-framework.patch (sg17)
    runtime cominterop.c: an array of structures (Guid[]) goes to COM as a
      SAFEARRAY(VT_RECORD), with an IRecordInfo for a blittable structure,
      and SAFEARRAY(VT_RECORD) comes back to T[], as .NET marshals them
      (Visual Studio's IVsUIContextManagerPrivate.InvokeCallbackUnderLock:
      "Failed to create SafeArray"). An element conversion that throws no
      longer cleans its error twice (CLEANUP_CALLED_SENTINEL assert);
      SafeArrayDestroy's DISP_E_ARRAYISLOCKED in marshal cleanup does not
      fail the call; a CCW's GetIDsOfNames with a NULL name is
      DISP_E_UNKNOWNNAME (was a giconv NULL assertion).
    corlib AppDomainSetup.TargetFrameworkName gets a setter, kept outside
      the instance so the layout the runtime shares stays the same
      (VsAppDomainManager.InitializeNewDomain: MissingMethodException).
    Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg17 section); the sg16 build fails its
      three new checks.

mono-0007-apartment-state-ccw-comvisible-qi.patch (sg18)
    corlib Thread.GetApartmentState / ApartmentState: a thread with no
      apartment state of its own (one that came into managed code from
      native code) reports the COM apartment it is in (CoGetApartmentType),
      as .NET does. Visual Studio's devenv makes its main thread an STA and
      WPF said "The calling thread must be STA" (WindowManagerService's type
      initializer).
    runtime cominterop.c: a CCW's QueryInterface for a managed interface
      marked [ComVisible(false)] is E_NOINTERFACE, as on .NET, not a CCW
      with no methods. Visual Studio's shell QueryService()s with a service
      id (an empty [ComVisible(false)] marker interface) as the IID, called
      slot 5 of what came back and crashed writing into msenv.
    Both from gaps2.
    Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg18 section); the sg17 build fails it.

mono-0008-debugger-agent-dt-unix.patch (sg20)
    The soft debugger agent's one transport in Wine Mono is dt_unix: the
    user's own Unix socket ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wine-mono-debug/ADDRESS.sock,
    0700 directory, 0600 socket), opened in the program's process through
    Wine's sgdbgsock.dll (wine-sg 7791), the peer's uid checked
    (SO_PEERCRED) before a byte is read. dt_socket and socket-fd are not
    registered on HOST_WIN32: no TCP at all (David 2026-11-15: option c --
    a loopback port with an unauthenticated handshake is cross-user code
    execution on a shared machine). server=y,suspend=n waits for the attach
    in the debugger thread, which ICorDebug's DebugActiveProcess needs
    (wine-sg 7792/7796).

mono-0009-safearray-array-out-ignoresaccesschecks.patch (sg20)
    runtime marshal.c/cominterop.c: a System.Array declared as a SAFEARRAY
      of any element type goes through the SAFEARRAY marshaller (it went to
      the object marshaller and came back empty unless the subtype was
      VARIANT); with no subtype it is SAFEARRAY(VARIANT). Visual Studio's
      IVsSettingsStore2.GetSubCollectionNames returns SAFEARRAY(VT_BSTR) as
      Array: the brokered-service registry came back empty, nothing was
      registered, and VSGlobalBrokeredServiceContainer.Proffer threw
      "Cannot proffer unregistered service(s)".
    runtime cominterop.c: an [Out]-only SAFEARRAY parameter gets its own
      local (NULL first); every one used local 0, so with two of them
      (GetPropertyNamesAndValues) the first came back as the second:
      SafeArrayTypeMismatchException in the first-launch window's colours.
    runtime class.c + custom-attrs.c, corlib AssemblyBuilder: a dynamic
      assembly carrying [IgnoresAccessChecksTo("Name")] may reach anything
      in assembly Name, as on .NET Framework (ServiceHub's/StreamJsonRpc's
      Reflection.Emit proxies call internal helpers: MethodAccessException
      and a native crash). AssemblyBuilder.SetCustomAttribute hands that
      attribute to the runtime, and an internal attribute type of another
      module of the same assembly is visible to it.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg20 section); sg18 fails
      its three checks.

mono-0010-vs-ide-com-events-arrays-eventprovider.patch (sg21)
    runtime cominterop.c/marshal-ilgen.c (+ jit-icall-reg.h,
      icall-signatures.h): an array of COM interfaces
      ([MarshalAs(LPArray, ArraySubType = Interface)] IFoo[]) in a call
      from native code to managed code is marshaled both ways: interface
      pointers become objects (Mono's own CCWs their object, others an RCW),
      and an [Out] array's objects go back as owned interface pointers. An
      interface counted as a blittable type of size 0, so nothing was
      copied: Visual Studio's msenv called IEnumWindowFrames.Next on a
      managed enumerator, got S_OK and a NULL frame, and crashed.
    runtime cominterop.c + corlib ComInteropProxy/__ComObject: a call to a
      [ComEventInterface] interface on a COM object goes to the interface's
      event provider class, made once per object with the object, as .NET
      does (connection points). Visual Studio's SolutionNavigatorPackage
      subscribes to DTE CommandEvents.BeforeExecute and failed SetSite with
      E_NOINTERFACE (an InvalidCastException).
    System.Core: System.Diagnostics.Eventing.EventProvider and
      EventDescriptor, with no ETW session listening (IsEnabled false,
      writes succeed). Visual Studio's SccProviderPackage keeps one in Team
      Foundation's trace settings; the class failed to load.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21 sections: outarr, comevent,
      eventprovider); sg18/sg20 fail all three.

mono-0011-rcw-takes-its-reference-first.patch (sg21)
    corlib ComInteropProxy.GetProxy: a new runtime callable wrapper takes its
      own reference (QueryInterface in __ComObject's constructor) before
      GetProxy releases the one it took to look the object up. A native
      caller may hand over an object nobody holds yet (count 0): Visual
      Studio's msenv advises a new solution events sink that way. Released
      first, the sink deleted itself, the wrapper kept a dangling pointer,
      and the cast to IVsSolutionEvents crashed in QueryInterface.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21 rcwref section); sg18 and
      sg20 crash in it.

mono-0012-ccw-release-with-no-reference-left.patch (sg21)
    metadata/cominterop.c cominterop_ccw_release: a Release of a COM callable
      wrapper that has no reference left no longer asserts (process abort);
      it warns which managed class it was (once; every time with
      SG_DEBUG_COMQI), returns 0 and leaves the count at 0, as .NET's retail
      runtime lets the caller's extra Release pass. Visual Studio 2022 ended
      at startup on it (the wrapper of VSTelemetrySession).
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21 ccwover section); sg20
      aborts in it.

mono-0013-type-equivalence-of-embedded-interop-types.patch (sg21)
    metadata/metadata.c: type equivalence (NoPIA) in signature comparison, as
      .NET has it. Two interfaces or value types with the same namespace and
      name are one type in a method signature when both are [TypeIdentifier]
      or COM imports, at least one is [TypeIdentifier], and their [Guid]s, if
      any, are equal. A program built with "embed interop types" carries its
      own copy of such an interface; a call into another assembly that has
      its own copy failed with MissingMethodException. Visual Studio 2022's
      extension manager (SetupEngineHelpers.GetApplicationName taking
      ISetupInstance2) failed this way, and with it the MEF component model:
      the editor package and every MEF service were unavailable.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21 nopia section, ilasm);
      sg20 throws MissingMethodException. Mutant SG_MUTANT_NO_TYPE_EQUIVALENCE.

mono-0014-rcw-casts-of-a-proxy-made-for-an-interface.patch (sg21)
    corlib ComInteropProxy.CanCastTo: uses the proxy's own __ComObject.
      "o as __ComObject" on the transparent proxy of an RCW made for an
      interface asked CanCastTo again, until the stack overflowed (Visual
      Studio 2022's UI thread, at startup). Every RCW casts to __ComObject
      and its bases. Marshal.GetTypedObjectForIUnknown asks the RCW itself
      for the type's interfaces (through a proxy made for an interface,
      __ComObject's methods went to remoting, which threw).
      ComInteropProxy.ComObject (internal) gives runtime code the RCW.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21 rcwitf); sg20 overflows
      the stack.

mono-0015-cleanup-unused-objects-is-a-no-op.patch (sg21)
    corlib Marshal.CleanupUnusedObjectsInCurrentContext: nothing to do (RCWs
      are released when collected), instead of throwing
      PlatformNotSupportedException on Windows. Visual Studio 2022 calls it
      from its idle loop (IVsRCWCleanupPrivate), over a thousand times at
      startup.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21 cleanup); sg20 throws.

mono-0016-blocking-thread-whose-preempt-failed-counts-as-suspended.patch (sg21)
    mono/utils/mono-threads-windows.c: in hybrid suspend, when the preemptive
      suspend of a thread in a blocking (GC-safe) region fails (SuspendThread
      or GetThreadContext fails: in a shared prefix the machine's server
      reaches a user's threads only through that user's sg-procagent, and with
      the agent gone wine-sg 7843 fails the request), the request stays and the
      thread counts as suspended at the state it saved entering the region (as
      full cooperative suspend does); it is marked cooperative-suspend aware.
      Before, the abort put a BLOCKING thread to RUNNING: "Cannot transition
      thread ... from RUNNING with DONE_BLOCKING" and the program spun at 100%
      CPU (MSBuild, any .NET program, at its next collection).
    From gaps3. Gate: wine-sg test/agentgone-gate.sh (the .NET program with its
      agent killed mid-run); mutant SG_MUTANT_PREEMPT_FAIL_ABORTS.

mono-0017-typed-rcw-for-an-interface-is-the-objects-own-rcw.patch (sg21)
    corlib Marshal.GetTypedObjectForIUnknown: for an interface type it returns
      the object's own runtime callable wrapper (GetObjectForIUnknown: same
      identity, cast to the interface by QueryInterface), not a proxy made for
      that interface. Calls through such a proxy went to remoting and threw
      "The method or operation is not implemented": Visual Studio 2022's
      licensing state (IVsClientRightsTokenCacheManagerInitialize.Initialize)
      failed, and the IDE shut down after two minutes with "The evaluation
      period has expired" (devenv /Build: "Invalid license data").
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg21h section); stock 9.4.0
      overflows the stack, sg21 throws on the call.

mono-0018-interface-pointers-a-native-caller-passes-that-are-no-object-are-null.patch (sg22)
    mono/metadata/cominterop.c, native-to-managed calls (COM callable wrappers) only,
      interface-typed parameters only: a pointer that is misaligned, or whose object or
      vtable is not readable memory (VirtualQuery), is passed as null, with one warning per
      call site. Real objects are aligned and readable: valid calls see no change.
      Why: Visual Studio's own interop declares IPropertyBag.Read (the standard IID
      55272a00-...) with RemoteRead's 5 parameters (..., uint VARTYPE, [IUnknown] object
      pUnkObj); native callers pass the 3-argument Read, so pUnkObj is whatever their stack
      held (here 0x7f44eaec1afb). Mono called QueryInterface on it and devenv crashed
      opening a solution. The bug is in VS's declaration; .NET Framework's handling of it is
      unproven -- this is a defensive guard against bad callers.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg22 sg21i section: misaligned, unmapped,
      bad-vtable pointers arrive as null; sg21 crashes). Mutant SG_MUTANT_ITF_GUARD_OFF.

mono-0019-out-string-arrays-native-fills-come-back.patch (sg23)
    mono/metadata/marshal-ilgen.c, array marshalling, the way back (CONV_OUT) of a string array
      passed [Out] or [In, Out]: each native string is converted into the managed array
      (BSTR, LPWStr, the other string encodings) before it is freed; BSTR elements are freed
      with SysFreeString (mono_free_bstr), not the task allocator (an invalid free before).
      LPWStr elements keep Mono's no-free (they may be the managed strings' own buffers).
      Why: Visual Studio's managed SettingsManagerWrapper calls the native
      IVsSettingsManager.GetCommonExtensionsSearchPaths([In] uint, [Out, LPArray of BStr,
      SizeParamIndex 0] string[], out uint) with a sized array; Mono freed the paths native
      wrote and left the array of nulls, so the extension manager had no CommonExtensions
      root, no CPS MEF parts loaded and the C++ project system did not either (gaps3's VS
      notes, 2026-11-17).
    From gaps2. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg23 section: [Out] BSTR, [Out] LPWStr, [In, Out]
      BSTR arrays filled by a native COM method; sg22 gives null|null).

mono-0020-rcw-interfaces-released-outside-the-com-lock.patch (sg24)
    mono/metadata/cominterop.c, mono_System_ComObject_ReleaseInterfaces (a wrapper's last
      release: Marshal.FinalReleaseComObject, ReleaseComObject to 0, the finalizer): the
      wrapper is unlinked under the COM lock (rcw table, its interface table and IUnknown
      detached), and its interfaces are released after the lock is let go; a second release
      that comes in meanwhile finds nothing to release.
      Why: a release that waits -- a proxy's, calling into another apartment or process --
      held the lock, so every thread that needed it waited too. Visual Studio's UI thread
      waited in Marshal.GetCCW while its finalizer's release of a proxy waited
      (ClientRpcChannelBuffer_SendReceive): the Start window drew and never answered a click.
    From gaps2. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg24 section: a native object whose last
      release waits for the main thread's signal; the main thread takes the lock meanwhile;
      sg23 deadlocks). Mutant SG_MUTANT_RELEASE_UNDER_LOCK.

corefx-0003-end-wait-for-connection-on-a-closed-pipe.patch (sg25)
    System.Core, NamedPipeServerStream.EndWaitForConnection: a server that was closed
      (disposed) is ObjectDisposedException ("Cannot access a closed pipe"), checked
      before the wait's own result, as the .NET Framework's EndWaitForConnection does --
      also when the client's connection came in before the close. Mono's returned
      normally then (and threw IOException 0x2a4, ERROR_HANDLES_CLOSED, for a wait the
      close ended). A connection that completes after the close leaves the server closed
      (it was set Connected again).
      Why: callbacks written for the Framework catch that exception and stop; they went
      on to use the pipe that was gone. Visual Studio's response-time channel
      (ChannelReceiver.channelConnectedCallback) then threw an unhandled
      ObjectDisposedException from SafePipeHandle and the IDE exited a minute after start.
    From gaps2. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg25 section: connected then disposed,
      disposed while waiting, a plain connection); sg24 fails the first and third checks (76/78).

mono-0021-type-metadata-tokens-as-the-clrs.patch (sg26)
    mono/metadata/reflection.c, Type.MetadataToken (mono_reflection_get_token_checked, a
      RuntimeType): as the CLR's RuntimeTypeHandle::GetToken, an array, a pointer or a byref
      type is the nil TypeDef token 0x02000000 (Mono said 0 for arrays and pointers and the
      element's token for a byref), and a generic parameter is its GenericParam row
      0x2Annnnnn (Mono said 0).
      Why: Visual Studio's MEF cache writer (vs-mef SerializationContextBase.
      WriteCompressedMetadataToken) refuses a type token that is no TypeDef ("Wrong type.
      Parameter name: type"), so the IDE had no MEF catalog: SComponentModel unavailable,
      "Create a new project" listed no templates, packages failed SetSite.
    From gaps2. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg26 section); sg25 fails its token checks (sg25: 79/87 of the whole gate).
      Mutant SG_MUTANT_TYPE_TOKEN_ZERO.

mono-0022-proxy-settings-of-a-user-without-internet-settings.patch (sg26)
    System, AutoWebProxyScriptEngine.InitializeRegistryGlobalProxy: a user with no
      Internet Settings key (Registry.GetValue is then null) or values of another type has
      no proxy, as .NET Framework reads it; it threw NullReferenceException from every
      WebRequest.Create (Visual Studio's remote settings, sgtest1's fresh profile).
    From gaps2. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg26 section, the proxy check).

mono-0023-com-aggregation-create-aggregated-object.patch (sg26)
    corlib Marshal.CreateAggregatedObject (it threw NotImplementedException) and COM
      aggregation in mono/metadata/cominterop.c: the managed object's COM callable
      wrapper gets the outer object's IUnknown and an inner (non-delegating) IUnknown,
      which CreateAggregatedObject returns with one reference. Every interface of the
      wrapper delegates QueryInterface/AddRef/Release to the outer object; the inner
      IUnknown answers QueryInterface for the managed object's interfaces (IUnknown is
      itself) and hands them out with an outer reference, as COM aggregation does;
      its IMarshal is a free-threaded marshaler the outer object controls.
      Why: Visual Studio's DTE.Solution wraps its managed solution object so
      (ComWrapperFactory.CreateAggregatedObject); DTE2.get_Solution returned E_NOTIMPL
      and Next in "Create a new project" ended the IDE.
    From gaps2. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg26 section: a native outer object
      aggregating a managed one, seven checks); sg25 throws NotImplementedException.
      The IMarshal check caught a first draft that took the marshaler for a wrapper
      interface (a crash in Wine's ftmarshaler_Release, as Visual Studio crashed).

mono-0024-a-frame-of-emitted-code-printed-without-a-guid.patch (sg27)
    mono/metadata/metadata.c mono_guid_to_string_minimal: a NULL GUID is printed as all
      zeros. An image made by Reflection.Emit (AssemblyBuilder) has no #GUID heap, and
      Mono's native frame printing (mono_debug_print_stack_frame: WINE_MONO_TRACE=E:all,
      thread dumps) read its module id through NULL.
      Why: every exception-traced Visual Studio run died about 3 minutes after start
      (page fault reading address 3 in libmono), so its exceptions could not be traced.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg27 section: emitted code throws three
      times under WINE_MONO_TRACE=E:all); sg26 dies. Mutant SG_MUTANT_DYNAMIC_GUID_NULL.

mono-0025-safearrays-by-reference-into-managed-com-methods.patch (sg28)
    mono/metadata/cominterop.c, the SAFEARRAY marshaler of a native-to-managed call (a COM
      callable wrapper's method): a SAFEARRAY* passed by reference (ref object[]) arrives as
      the array (NULL as null; it threw MarshalDirectiveException "Byref input safearray
      marshaling not implemented"), and one that is [Out] or in/out (ref with no direction, or
      [In, Out]) goes back as the array the method left, the caller's own destroyed first;
      an [In] ref one stays the caller's.
      Why: Visual Studio calls the C++ project wizards (Microsoft.VC.Wizards, managed) through
      EnvDTE.IDTWizard.Execute with its context and custom parameters by reference; the
      wrapper threw before the wizard ran, DTE reported OLE_E_PROMPTSAVECANCELLED
      (0x8004000C) and Create in "Create a new project" made no project.
    From gaps3. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg28 section: the managed object's wrapper
      called through its vtable with SAFEARRAY**: in, [In], ref, NULL); sg27 0x80131535.
      Mutant SG_MUTANT_BYREF_SAFEARRAY_IN.

mono-0026-bool-arrays-in-com-methods-as-variant-bool.patch (sg29)
    mono/metadata/marshal-ilgen.c, LPArray marshaling of bool[] (both directions, managed to
      native and native to managed): each element is sized and converted by ArraySubType --
      I1/U1 one byte, VariantBool two bytes with -1 for true, otherwise a four-byte BOOL --
      where Mono always used a four-byte BOOL and ignored ArraySubType, and a native-to-
      managed bool[] (a COM callable wrapper's method) threw MarshalDirectiveException
      ("non-blittable arrays to managed code is not implemented"). mono/metadata/cominterop.c:
      in COM interface methods (RCW and CCW wrappers) a bool[] with no ArraySubType is
      VARIANT_BOOL, as in .NET; P/Invoke keeps BOOL.
      Why: Visual Studio's MEF host asks IVsAppQueryLoad2.QueryLoadComponentAssemblies which
      component assemblies may load ([Out] bool[] of VARIANT_BOOLs). Read as four-byte
      BOOLs, only the first half of the 222 assemblies came back approved, so VS never
      scanned the core editor's MEF parts (Microsoft.VisualStudio.Platform.VSEditor and
      the rest): no ITextDocumentFactoryService, and the C++ wizards' Create failed with
      "Exception of type 'System.Exception' was thrown".
    From gaps3b. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg29 section: a native object filling and
      reading bool arrays through an RCW, and native code calling a managed object's
      wrapper: default, I1 and Bool elements); sg28 gives 111000 for 101101 and the wrapper
      0x80131535. Mutant SG_MUTANT_COM_BOOL_ARRAY_BOOL.

mono-0027-vs-cpp-project-system-loads.patch (sg30)
    Four fixes that Visual Studio 2022's C++ project system needed to load and create a project:
    - mcs/class/corlib System.Reflection.Emit TypeBuilder/AssemblyBuilder: a dynamic assembly
      carrying [IgnoresAccessChecksTo("Name")] may implement assembly Name's private nested
      interfaces, as on .NET Framework. vs-mef's metadata view proxies do that
      (ProjectMultiThreadedService+IJoinableTaskContextProviderMetadataView); TypeBuilder threw
      TypeLoadException "implements the inaccessible interface", so CPS's IProjectService had no
      export and the Visual C++ Project System package failed (SetSite 0x80131522).
    - mono/metadata/marshal-ilgen.c, native-to-managed wrappers: [Out] on a by-value primitive,
      IntPtr or string copies nothing back, as in .NET; Mono aborted building the wrapper
      ("should not be reached") -- IVCManagedInterop has [In, Out] IntPtr rgText, and VS quit
      when it made that object's COM wrapper.
    - mono/metadata/cominterop.c cominterop_set_ccw_domain: a native caller using a wrapper
      whose object was collected gets an error HRESULT (warning once) instead of Mono reading
      through NULL (VS crashed in IVsTask.get_IsCanceled now and then).
    - mcs/class/corlib System/Variant.cs: an object goes into a VARIANT as VT_DISPATCH when it
      answers QueryInterface(IDispatch), else VT_UNKNOWN, without throwing and catching
      InvalidCastException each time (1.4 million on VS's UI thread in five minutes).
    From gaps3b. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg30 section: emitted proxy of a private
      interface with and without the attribute; a wrapper with [In, Out] IntPtr; VARIANT types;
      a collected object's wrapper called). sg29: TypeLoadException, then Mono aborts.
      Mutants SG_MUTANT_IACT_PRIVATE_INTERFACE, SG_MUTANT_OUT_BYVAL_PRIMITIVE_ASSERTS,
      SG_MUTANT_CCW_DEAD_OBJECT_CRASHES, SG_MUTANT_VARIANT_DISPATCH_BY_EXCEPTION.

mono-0028-safearray-element-types-from-native.patch (sg31)
    mono/metadata/metadata.c (+ metadata.h): a SAFEARRAY marshal spec's SafeArrayUserDefinedSubType
      (the SerString after the variant type, ECMA-335 II.23.4) is read and kept; Mono read the
      string's first byte as an element count and dropped the type.
    mono/metadata/cominterop.c: a System.Array parameter declared SAFEARRAY(VT_UNKNOWN/
      VT_DISPATCH/VT_RECORD) with such a type comes from native code as an array of it; a
      System.Array with no element type at all comes as an array of what the SAFEARRAY holds
      (VT_UNKNOWN object[], VT_I4 int[], ...), as in .NET; Mono read both as VARIANT
      (object[], or SafeArrayTypeMismatchException).
      Why: Visual Studio's VCProjectEngine2.WaitForMultipleTasks takes SAFEARRAY(VT_UNKNOWN) of
      IVCTask as System.Array and casts it to IVCTask[]; as object[] every call threw
      InvalidCastException, the native caller retried for ever and "Creating project..." never
      ended (1.3 million exceptions on VS's UI thread).
    From gaps3b. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg31 section: a managed object's wrapper called
      with SAFEARRAYs of VT_UNKNOWN with a user-defined subtype whose name takes a two-byte
      length, and with no subtype of VT_UNKNOWN/VT_DISPATCH/VT_I4/VT_I8); sg30 gives Object[]
      and 0x80131533. Mutants SG_MUTANT_SAFEARRAY_UDT_IGNORED,
      SG_MUTANT_SYSTEM_ARRAY_SAFEARRAY_AS_VARIANT.

mono-0029-struct-arrays-unsized-arrays-and-records-into-managed.patch (sg32)
    mono/metadata/marshal-ilgen.c, native-to-managed (COM callable wrapper) arrays:
    - an LPArray with neither SizeConst nor SizeParamIndex is one element, as the CLR takes it
      (a pointer to one item); Mono made an empty array;
    - an array of non-blittable structs (strings in them) is converted element by element, in
      and out; Mono threw MarshalDirectiveException ("non-string and non-blittable arrays to
      managed code is not implemented").
    mcs/class/corlib System/Variant.cs: a VARIANT holding a record (VT_RECORD) becomes the
      managed value type whose [Guid] is the record's (IRecordInfo.GetGuid), or System.Guid
      for oleaut32's GUID record; Mono threw NotImplementedException.
    Why: Visual Studio's IVsFontAndColorDefaults.GetItem(int, AllColorableItemInfo[] pInfo)
      (430 failures while VS opened a new C++ project) and IVsGuidParserPrivate.TryParseGuid
      with a GUID record.
    From gaps3b. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg32 section: a wrapper filling one unsized
      struct with a BSTR and reading two sized ones; VT_RECORD of a [Guid] struct and of a GUID
      record through a fake IRecordInfo); sg31 0x80131535 / 0x80004001. Mutants
      SG_MUTANT_UNSIZED_ARRAY_EMPTY, SG_MUTANT_NO_STRUCT_ARRAYS_TO_MANAGED,
      SG_MUTANT_VARIANT_RECORD_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.

mono-0030-network-interface-ip-statistics.patch (sg33)
    mcs/class/System/System.Net.NetworkInformation: Win32NetworkInterface2 overrides
    GetIPStatistics (.NET Framework 4.6+): a fresh GetIfEntry of the interface each call, as
    .NET queries each time; Mono's base threw NotImplementedException. MIB_IFROW's counters
    are DWORDs and are now read unsigned in both statistics classes (Win32IPv4InterfaceStatistics
    returned negative byte counts past 2 GB).
    Why: LibreHardwareMonitor 0.9.6 died at start (Computer.IsNetworkEnabled -> Network ctor ->
      GetIPStatistics). With it, LHM's window and sensor tree come up (host build, sg32 + this).
    From apps8. Gate: test/mono-fork-test.sh (sg33 section, outbox mono-fork-test-sg33-hunk.sh):
      every interface's GetIPStatistics, no negative counters; sg32 NotImplementedException.
      Mutant SG_MUTANT_IPSTATS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.
    Verified: probe on sg32 image (old: NotImplementedException; new: 2/2 interfaces, enp4s0
      rx 2230148193 -- was -2071081991 before the unsigned read).
